WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.120
One mouse data set sorted sensory neurons into eleven kinds.

2
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.660
A larger one, three years later, found seventeen.

3
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:16.360
A 2024 merger of 31 data sets from six species settled on eighteen.

4
00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.080
Which number is right?

5
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.600
None of the three is wrong, and none is final.

6
00:00:21.633 --> 00:00:26.513
The dorsal root ganglion or (DRG) holds the cell bodies of sensory neurons.

7
00:00:27.213 --> 00:00:33.053
In 2015, Usoskin and colleagues sequenced the RNA of 622 single neurons from mouse

8
00:00:33.053 --> 00:00:37.353
ganglia. An unsupervised method grouped them without being told the answer.

9
00:00:38.253 --> 00:00:39.453
Eleven classes came out.

10
00:00:40.053 --> 00:00:44.853
Five were neurofilament-rich, three were non-peptidergic, two were peptidergic, and

11
00:00:44.853 --> 00:00:47.133
one was marked by tyrosine hydroxylase.

12
00:00:48.083 --> 00:00:52.783
Think of a map. A country-level map and a county-level map of the same land are both

13
00:00:52.783 --> 00:00:54.883
correct. They differ in resolution.

14
00:00:55.783 --> 00:01:01.043
Zeisel and colleagues, in 2018, resolved eight peptidergic, six non-peptidergic and

15
00:01:01.043 --> 00:01:02.363
three neurofilament types.

16
00:01:02.903 --> 00:01:03.903
That makes seventeen.

17
00:01:04.523 --> 00:01:08.863
More cells, deeper sequencing or a finer clustering setting can split branches.

18
00:01:10.017 --> 00:01:15.517
In 2024, Bhuiyan, Renthal and colleagues merged 31 data sets from six mammalian

19
00:01:15.517 --> 00:01:17.177
species into one reference.

20
00:01:17.817 --> 00:01:19.857
It holds eighteen DRG subtypes.

21
00:01:20.517 --> 00:01:25.057
The authors stressed that preparation, sequencing depth and analysis change how many

22
00:01:25.057 --> 00:01:26.157
subtypes are resolved.

23
00:01:26.817 --> 00:01:30.277
One group is defined by injury-induced transcription factors.

24
00:01:30.737 --> 00:01:32.177
They call it the Atf3 group.

25
00:01:32.177 --> 00:01:35.357
That is a warning that an atlas can mix types with states.

26
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.040
Can a state be told from a type?

27
00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.840
Renthal and colleagues cut or crushed peripheral nerves in mice, then sequenced

28
00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.660
single nuclei from the ganglion.

29
00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.860
Injured neurons lost much of the expression that marked their subtype.

30
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:54.100
Over a time course associated with axons regrowing to their targets, identity

31
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:58.600
re-emerged. Timing alone does not prove that regrowth restores identity.

32
00:01:59.683 --> 00:02:00.763
So what is a type?

33
00:02:01.143 --> 00:02:03.723
A molecular group that recurs across data sets.

34
00:02:04.243 --> 00:02:09.223
It also needs an independent anchor, such as an ending, a physiology, a target, or

35
00:02:09.223 --> 00:02:10.563
behaviour after manipulation.

36
00:02:11.543 --> 00:02:14.583
Even then, read it with its state and its resolution in mind.

37
00:02:15.323 --> 00:02:18.483
Read each count with its data set, resolution and state.
